AURIX TC375 blinky
==================

A minimal bare-metal image for the Infineon AURIX TC375 Lite Kit V2,
used to check end to end that OpenOCD can erase, program and verify the
program flash of an AURIX device, and that the programmed image really
runs from a cold boot with no debugger attached.

CPU0 alternates LED1 (P00.5) and LED2 (P00.6) at about 1 Hz.

Building
--------

Any TriCore ELF GCC works. Point CROSS_COMPILE at it:

    make CROSS_COMPILE=/path/to/bin/tricore-elf-

Flashing
--------

    openocd -f board/infineon/kit_a2g_tc375_lite.cfg \
            -c "program tc375-blinky.elf verify reset exit"

or simply

    make CROSS_COMPILE=... flash

The kit carries an on-board miniWiggler V3 probe, so no external debug
hardware is needed. Interface 0 (FTDI channel A) carries the DAP protocol
that OpenOCD claims directly over libusb; interface 1 (channel B) is a
plain UART bridge to the target and is meant to stay bound to ftdi_sio.
On some kernels ftdi_sio also binds interface 0 as a tty, which then
makes OpenOCD's libusb_claim_interface() fail. contrib/60-openocd.rules
already unbinds ftdi_sio from interface 0 on hotplug; if it is not
installed, do it once by hand before running OpenOCD:

    echo '<bus>-<port>:1.0' > /sys/bus/usb/drivers/ftdi_sio/unbind

After "reset exit" OpenOCD releases the target and the LEDs start
blinking. Power cycling the board must keep them blinking; if they stop,
the image was programmed but is not running from cold boot.

Hardware regression suite
-------------------------

../common/test-aurix-hardware.cfg is a regression suite for the
AURIX support that runs against the same kit. It covers memory access
across widths and alignments, flash erase, program and verify through
both the algorithm and the work-area-less fallback, reset, halt, resume
and single step, hardware breakpoints, data and range watchpoints, error
handling on faulted accesses, and sustained transfers that stress the
DAP response decoder. It finishes by running this image and sampling the
port register to confirm the LEDs really alternate.

Build the image first, then:

    openocd -f board/infineon/kit_a2g_tc375_lite.cfg \
            -f ../common/test-aurix-hardware.cfg

Each check prints PASS or FAIL, the suite prints a final tally and exits
non zero if anything failed, so it can be used directly as a pass/fail
gate. It is plain OpenOCD script with no external dependencies, so it
runs on every host OpenOCD builds for.

It is deliberately not wired into "make check", because it needs this
specific board and must never run as part of an ordinary build. The
hardware-free tests for the AURIX support live in testing/tcl_commands
and do run under "make check".

Notes
-----

The image is linked at 0xa0000000, the non-cached alias of program flash
bank 0, because that is where the factory default boot mode header in
the UCB points the reset vector. The TC375 board configuration declares
the physical bank in the cached alias and exposes the non-cached alias
with OpenOCD's "virtual" flash driver, so no address translation is
needed when programming and both views share one sector state.

The watchdogs are disabled in the reset handler before anything else
happens. The TC3xx boots with the CPU and safety watchdogs armed with a
window of roughly 128 ms, so an application that does not service them
resets the device long before the first LED transition is visible. This
is easy to miss during development, because an attached debugger
suspends the watchdogs and the same image then runs correctly after
"reset halt" followed by "resume".
